id: 1376
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1376, "run_id": "run_a3fbe594142b4e3e", "data": {"id": "a8bdff6c-7ec0-4e56-afbd-c9acd3524bca", "run_id": "run_a3fbe594142b4e3e", "kind": "tool", "position": 1374, "revision": 1376, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"limit\": 20, \"offset\": 80}", "status": "running", "tool_use_id": "call_eb51017c27184232a657d486"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:28:59.975379+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:29:00.663946+00:00"}}

id: 1378
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1378, "run_id": "run_a3fbe594142b4e3e", "data": {"id": "a8bdff6c-7ec0-4e56-afbd-c9acd3524bca", "run_id": "run_a3fbe594142b4e3e", "kind": "tool", "position": 1374, "revision": 1378, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"limit\": 20, \"offset\": 80}", "output": "80\t\n81\t**Note on sample filtering in clustered regressions:** The clustered standard error implementation (`working/subC/robustness_analysis.py`, lines ~247–252) excludes clusters with only one observation (groups where `group_sizes > 1`). This reduces the glacial sample from 427 to 324 (Reference clustering) and 350 (Locality clustering), and the fluvial sample from 6,128 to 6,109 (Reference) and 4,609 (Locality). All clustered results reported above use these filtered samples. The baseline OLS results use the full complete-case samples (glacial n=427, fluvial n=6,128, combined n=6,555).\n82\t\n83\t**Supporting evidence:** `output/robustness_results.json` (clustered_results and ols_vs_clustered_comparison sections); `working/subC/robustness_analysis.py`.\n84\t\n85\t**Robustness:** Outlier trimming (top/bottom 1%) reduces OLS slope magnitudes by ~50% but does not change direction. Winsorization produces similar results. The clustering finding is robust to outlier treatment because it reflects the fundamental non-independence structure of the data, not leverage points.\n86\t\n87\t**Counterevidence and limitations:** The clustered analysis reveals that the apparent timescale effect is driven by a small number of influential studies/sites rather than a broad empirical pattern across thousands of independent observations. Reference counts differ by sample口径: (a) 154 unique References in the full glacial dataset (427 rows, all rows; from `working/subA/audit_results.json`); (b) 248 unique References in the glacial complete-case subset used for log-space regression (427 rows, no time/erosion missing; from `output/robustness_results.json` source_site_clustering.unique_references); (c) 51 References with ≥2 observations retained after single-observation-cluster filtering in the clustered glacial regression (n_clusters=51, n_obs=324). For fluvial: (a) 265 unique References in the full raw dataset (6,288 rows); (b) 248 unique References in the complete-case subset (6,128 rows, excluding 4 zero-rate + 160 missing-time rows); (c) 229 References with ≥2 observations retained in the clustered fluvial regression (n_clusters=229, n_obs=6,109). The effective sample size after clustering is much smaller than the nominal count, limiting the generalizability of any timescale-erosion relationship derived from this compilation.\n88\t\n89\t**Falsification proposal:** Design a new field campaign targeting ≥50 geologically distinct sites spanning the full range of climates, lithologies, and process types, with each site measured using a single standardized methodology. If the timescale-erosion relationship holds with properly independent observations, the current null result would be overturned.\n90\t\n91\t---\n92\t\n93\t## Unresolved Questions and Alternative Explanations\n94\t\n95\t1. **Mechanical coupling cannot be fully disentangled.** The near -1.0 slopes for cosmogenic methods are consistent with rate = thickness/time, but we lack independent thickness measurements to test this directly. Without such data, we cannot definitively separate physical scale dependence from measurement artifact for these methods.\n96\t\n97\t2. **The Relief method reversal.** Fluvial Relief rates exceed glacial Relief rates, contrary to the overall pattern. This may reflect that Relief-based estimates capture long-term topographic evolution where fluvial incision in active orogens (e.g., Himalayas, Alps) produces high rates, while glacial Relief estimates often come from deglaciated areas with lower long-term rates. More data are needed.\n98\t\n99\t3. **OCTOPUS compilation lacks time intervals.** The OCTOPUS dataset (n=236 with erosion rates) provides distribution-level consistency checks but could not be included in regression analyses due to missing time interval columns.", "status": "completed", "is_async": false, "tool_use_id": "call_eb51017c27184232a657d486"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:28:59.975379+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:29:00.834930+00:00"}}

